From c20a4f9ed56631d55f5422f5720f90a5991a04c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrii Cherniavskyi <achernia@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Sat, 24 May 2025 23:24:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] gst-omx: dec: Fix flush/seek support.

Add changes to gstomxvideodec to support the modern way of flushing
the pipeline via FLUSH_START and FLUSH_STOP events, and fix deadlocks
that happen when the pipeline does a seek.

Most notable changes:
* Repurpose `self->started` variable: use it to check if decoder went
to PAUSED/PLAYING to make decisions whether  `handle_frame` is allowed
to proceed with acquiring input buffers, and whether `flush()` is
allowed to finish ports flushing.
* Drop frames via `gst_video_decoder_drop_frame()` instead of unref.
* Do a better dec_loop task management:
** Rely on task's state when draining instead of waiting for
drain_cond.
** Access `self->downstream_flow_ret` only if dec_loop is stopped to
prevent races, e.g. during an error flow. This permits removing some
decoder stream locks-unlocks.
* Do not rely on output port flushing state when doing buffer
reallocation (i.e. calling dec_enable()). Instead, introduce
`self->output_allocated` flag that's enabled when the decoder starts
and/or the format change happens.
* Support FLUSH_START event: set in/out ports to flushing state in the
event handler and wait until the main loop thread is stopped.
* Protect OMX flushing enabling and port buffer reallocation sequence
with a lock. The lock, while being potentially expensive, helps to
avoid dealing with problematic OMX behavior when the flush and caps
change happen at the same time.
* Do not push output buf if there is no corresponding frame. This can
happen when frames are being dropped during flushing. It's not
desirable to keep producing leftover output buffers after flushing is
done.
* Always wait for flushing completion OMX event to arrive. Do not
ignore flush completion event coming from OMX if port's flushing flag
was FALSE. OMX flush enabling and disabling calls are possible from
separate threads, i.e. one thread can start flushing and wait for its
completion with port lock released, whereas another thread will
disable the flushing. The first thread still has to be informed that
its flushing operation was completed.

Signed-off-by: Andrii Cherniavskyi <achernia@qti.qualcomm.com>

